{"duration_api_ms":109304,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"ae62b545-51d4-4aef-b2da-a716d8d05a00","total_cost_usd":0.099137,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11841,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":15089,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":12811},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11841,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":10,"output_tokens":15089,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11841},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":15089,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11841,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.099137,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":12811,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# BIP: 39\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nThis BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence — a group of easy to remember words — for the generation of deterministic wallets. It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed. This seed can be later used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\\n\\n## Copyright\\n\\nThis BIP falls under the GPL License.\\n\\n## Motivation\\n\\nA mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of wallet assets. The sentence could be written on paper or spoken over the telephone. This guide is a way to transport computer-generated determinism with human-readable transcription. It is not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\\n\\n## Generating the Mnemonic\\n\\nThe mnemonic must encode entropy in multiples of 32 bits. With more entropy, security is improved but the sentence length increases. The initial entropy length is referred to as ENT. The allowed size of ENT is 128–256 bits.\\n\\nFirst, an initial entropy of ENT bits is generated. A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash. This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy. Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0–2047, serving as an index into a wordlist. Finally, these numbers are converted into words, and the joined words form the mnemonic sentence.\\n\\nThe following table describes the relationship between the initial entropy length (ENT), the checksum length (CS), and the length of the generated mnemonic sentence (MS) in words.\\n\\n```\\nCS = ENT / 32\\nMS = (ENT + CS) / 11\\n\\n|  ENT  | CS | ENT+CS |  MS  |\\n+-------+----+--------+------+\\n|  128  |  4 |   132  |  12  |\\n|  160  |  5 |   165  |  15  |\\n|  192  |  6 |   198  |  18  |\\n|  224  |  7 |   231  |  21  |\\n|  256  |  8 |   264  |  24  |\\n```\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0039.mediawiki\",\n  \"decisions\": [],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a3\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence — a group of easy to remember words — for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Combined with a4–a5 in abstract; corrected errors noted in additions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Combined with a3, a5 in abstract; seed type corrected in additions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"This seed can be later used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Combined with a3–a4 in abstract; content unchanged.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a9\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of wallet assets.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Clarity; satoshis changed to assets for generality.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a11\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"This guide is a way to transport computer-generated determinism with human-readable transcription.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Clarity; removed meant to be for directness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a12\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"It is not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Grammar and spelling; It's expanded, süeed corrected.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b1\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"## Generating the Mnemonic\",\n      \"reason\": \"Heading; memonic corrected to Mnemonic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b2\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The mnemonic must encode entropy in multiples of 32 bits.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Typo and factual errors corrected; see additions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The initial entropy length is referred to as ENT.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Passive voice for consistency.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The allowed size of ENT is 128–256 bits.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Range and unit corrected per BIP39 spec; see additions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b7\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Hash function corrected; SHA652 → SHA256.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b8\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Checksum position corrected per BIP39 spec.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b9\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0–2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Bit group size corrected; 12 → 11 bits.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b10\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Finally, these numbers are converted into words, and the joined words form the mnemonic sentence.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Passive voice and clarity; joined words form completes thought.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b11\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The following table describes the relationship between the initial entropy length (ENT), the checksum length (CS), and the length of the generated mnemonic sentence (MS) in words.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Word choice; relation → relationship.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\",\n  \"additions\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"for the generation of deterministic wallets\",\n      \"corrects\": \"for the generation of deterministic UTOXs\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"BIP39 generates wallets and seeds, not transaction outputs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"into a binary seed\",\n      \"corrects\": \"into a ASCII seed\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"PBKDF2 derives a binary seed, not ASCII-encoded.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"in multiples of 32 bits\",\n      \"corrects\": \"in a multiple of 23 bits\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"BIP39 spec requires entropy in multiples of 32 bits.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The allowed size of ENT is 128–256 bits.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"The allowed size of ENT is 1024-2048 byts.\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"BIP39 allows 128–256 bits; 1024–2048 is incorrect.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"its SHA256 hash\",\n      \"corrects\": \"its SHA652 hash\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"SHA256 is the hash function used; SHA652 does not exist.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"appended to the end of the initial entropy\",\n      \"corrects\": \"appended to the start of the initial entropy\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"BIP39 appends checksum after entropy, not before.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"split into groups of 11 bits\",\n      \"corrects\": \"split into groups of 12 bits\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"2^11 = 2048 matches the wordlist index range.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms":97643,"type":"result","duration_ms":109369,"uuid":"185cd9d2-d7d2-4b23-a13e-4ced180f8cac","ttft_stream_ms":1431,"time_to_request_ms":63,"first_content_frame_ms":1431,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
